  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current program uses coordinate descent and subgradient methods to minimize the overlap integral. However, we need a more efficient symmetry-preserving optimization. We'll use a multi-resolution Bayesian optimization framework to explore the state space more effectively. The central idea is to (1) enforce symmetry to halve the degrees of freedom, (2) use Bayesian optimization to navigate the reduced space, and (3) combine with local coordinate descent for fine tuning. This addresses three key bottlenecks: 1) Limited exploration through fixed-grid optimization, 2) Slow convergence due to gradient-based methods, and 3) Inability to escape local optima.\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 \u211d^{n} (step function), with n being the grid points.\n- dx = 2.0 / n\n- Symmetry constraint: h[i] + h[n-1-i] = 1 for all i\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1\n\nRepresentation:\n- Use a 1D grid with linear interpolation\n- Enforce symmetry before each optimization step\n- Objective function: np.correlate(1e3*(h-0.5), mode='full') * dx (scaled for numerical stability)\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with initial_h_values from parent\n   - Enforce symmetry and mass conservation\n   - Use a coarse grid (n=8) for Bayesian exploration\n\n2. Bayesian Optimization phase:\n   - Use SLSQP with symmetry enforcement in dual space\n   - Candidate vectors: h1[:n_lowres//2] (then mirror/hard constraints)\n   - Acquisition function: Expected Improvement (EI) from cheap surrogate (SVR)\n   - Optimization steps n_lowres//2 at a time\n   - Budget: 600 seconds\n\n3. Coordinate Descent phase:\n   - Use anisotropic scaling projection with momentum\n   - Local search from Bayesian posters\n   - Budget: 400 seconds\n\n4. Final polishing:\n   - Up-sampling to target grid (n\u2264400)\n   - Symmetry-preserving interpolation\n   - Symmetry-enforced SLSQP with high alpha\n\n5. Boundary handling:\n   - Ensure symmetry at all stages: f[i] + f[-1-i] = 1 + noise(\u03b5)\n   - Use projection for integral: mass = n/2 + 10 (float tolerance)\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Core variations: (a) Initial grid size, (b) Anisotropic scaling factor, (c) Interpolation quality\n- Bayesian steering: Switch to gradient boosting if improvement plateau\n- Local search: Toggle QUOTA mode based on curvature\n\nComputational budget\n- Coarse grid: 8 points, budget 150 seconds\n- Exploration grids: 2 x 16 = 51 points, budget 200 seconds\n- Optimization phase: 4 rounds, budget 550 seconds\n- Final grid: n=400, budget 400 seconds\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Check symmetry within 1e-4 tolerance\n- Verify integral conservation \n- Re-calculate final score with independent evaluator\n- Must return (h, C5, n) with format:\n   h: np.array of length n\n   C5: float score\n   n: integer grid size",
